### Why is synchronizing on a String literal a bug?

String literals are interned by the JVM. Two classes using `"ACCOUNT_LOCK"` as a lock object are synchronizing on the same instance in memory.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class AccountService {
    public void debit() {
        synchronized ("ACCOUNT_LOCK") { // interned String
            // process debit
        }
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.report;

public class ReportService {
    public void generateReport() {
        synchronized ("ACCOUNT_LOCK") { // same interned String!
            // takes 30 seconds to generate report
        }
    }
}
```

While the report generates (30 seconds), ALL account debits are BLOCKED. These classes do not even know about each other.

### How can you prove that string literals are the same object?

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class StringInterningProof {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "LOCK";
        String b = "LOCK";
        System.out.println("Same object? " + (a == b)); // true

        String c = new String("LOCK");
        System.out.println("Same object? " + (a == c)); // false (new instance)

        String d = c.intern();
        System.out.println("Same object? " + (a == d)); // true (interned)
    }
}
```

```text
Same object? true
Same object? false
Same object? true
```

### What other objects have this problem?

`Boolean.TRUE`, `Boolean.FALSE`, and cached `Integer` values (-128 to 127) are shared singletons.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CachedObjectBugs {
    public void method1() {
        synchronized (Boolean.TRUE) { } // shared globally!
    }

    public void method2() {
        synchronized (Integer.valueOf(1)) { } // cached, shared!
    }

    // Both methods above use globally shared lock objects.
    // Any code in the JVM using the same object is blocked.
}
```

### What is the correct fix?

Use a `private final Object` as the lock. It is unique to your class and cannot be accessed or reassigned.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SafeAccountService {
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public void debit(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            // Private: no external code can lock on this
            // Final: cannot be reassigned to a different object
            // new Object(): unique, not shared with anyone
        }
    }
}
```

### What other anti-patterns should you avoid?

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LockAntiPatterns {
    // WRONG: synchronized(this) exposes the lock
    public synchronized void riskyMethod() {
        // External code can do: synchronized(myService) { sleep(60000); }
        // Blocks ALL your synchronized methods for 60 seconds!
    }

    // WRONG: non-final lock can be reassigned
    private Object lock = new Object(); // NOT final!
    public void fragileMethod() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            lock = new Object(); // reassigned! Next caller locks on a different object!
        }
    }

    // WRONG: new Object() inside synchronized
    public void brokenMethod() {
        synchronized (new Object()) { // new lock every call: no synchronization!
        }
    }
}
```

### How do IDEs and tools help?

IntelliJ IDEA warns: "Synchronization on a non-final field" and "Synchronization on interned String". SonarQube flags `synchronized("...")` as a critical bug (rule S1860). These checks catch the problem at development time.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **String.intern() memory**: in Java 7+, interned strings are stored in the heap (not PermGen), so they are garbage-collected when unreferenced. But string literals are always reachable from the constant pool.
- **Lock object identity**: the lock's identity (memory address) is what matters, not its value. Two different `new Object()` instances are different locks even though they have the same `toString()` output.
- **Kotlin val**: in Kotlin, `val lock = Any()` is already final. The language prevents the reassignment bug by default.
